   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-28",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777091",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-29",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Senator Abetz moved that the bills be read a second time, and Senator Murray moved an amendment to the motion concerning the Independent Contractors Bill 2006."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777092",
   "speaker": "Guy Barnett",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-29",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The speaker argued that the Independent Contractors Bill 2006 and related legislation would protect the right of workers to choose independent contracting, override state laws that deem contractors as employees, and maintain protections for outworkers and owner-drivers in New South Wales and Victoria. The speaker also claimed the bills would deliver on a 2004 election commitment, with $15 million in funding for enforcement, and that the government’s workplace reforms had increased jobs and real wages by 16.5% over 10 years."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777263",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-12-01",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777264",
   "speaker": "Eric Abetz",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-12-01",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The government moved amendments to the Independent Contractors Bill to clarify that state unfair contract laws would be excluded, and to remove proposed minimum remuneration and record-keeping protections for textile outworkers, arguing these were unnecessary. The government also introduced stand-down provisions and preserved redundancy entitlements for 12 months after agreement termination, while the opposition and crossbench contended the changes failed to protect vulnerable workers and bypassed proper Senate scrutiny."
  },
